# XSH aio_return()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XSH.aio_return.001
anchor     functions/aio_return.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/aio_return.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       After completion aio_return() gives the count write() or read()
           would: 5 for a 5-byte aio_write(), and 7 for an aio_read() of 10
           bytes from a file with 7 bytes.
test       t/001-count.c

assertion  XSH.aio_return.002
anchor     functions/aio_return.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/aio_return.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       For an aio_write() to a pipe with no reader that has failed,
           aio_return() gives -1, as write() would.
note       A failure already reported by the call leaves no return status
           to examine; the test is then UNRESOLVED.
test       t/002-failed.c

assertion  XSH.aio_return.003
anchor     functions/aio_return.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       After the status of a read has been retrieved, the same control
           block can be used for another request, and aio_return() then
           gives that request's status (2 for a 2-byte read).
test       t/003-reuse.c

assertion  XSH.aio_return.004
anchor     functions/aio_return.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
anchor     functions/aio_return.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       A second aio_return() for a request whose status was retrieved
           is made after the first returned the right count; the call must
           return, and whatever it returns is accepted.
note       The page says an error may be returned and the value of a call
           that does not fail is unspecified, and an implementation may add
           errors of its own, so neither a value nor an errno is required.
nomutant   the outcome is unspecified; there is nothing to plant
test       t/004-twice.c
